    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im studying for my security+ exam and i'm a bit confused. i get that hashing turns a password into a string of characters, but why do we actually need to add a 'salt' to it? like if the hash is already unique, why does the extra step matter?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Adding a salt to hashes, particularly passwords, helps enhance security by making each hash unique, even if two users have the same password. This prevents attackers from using precomputed hash tables like rainbow tables to crack the hashes. Using a salt also protects against dictionary attacks and ensures that even similar passwords produce different hashes, adding an extra layer of complexity to password protection. It is an essential step in securely storing passwords and protecting user data.",
      "source": "target",
