    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay that makes sense, but i'm still struggling with the rainbow table part. can you explain exactly how the salt stops a rainbow table from working? like, what is actually happening to the table during the attack if a salt is present?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! A rainbow table is a precomputed table of hash-ciphertext pairs used to reverse hash functions, primarily for cracking passwords. When you've added a salt (a random value), even if two users have the same password, their resultant hashes will differ because the included salt changes each time.\n\nDuring an attack, the attacker would have to factor in the salt alongside the password, because the salt alters the resulting hash. Therefore, an attacker would need to generate a new rainbow table for each potential salt value, vastly expanding the amount of precomputation needed, if not rendering it impractical. As a result, the presence of a salt dramatically increases the difficulty and time required to crack passwords using rainbow tables.",
      "source": "target",
